WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.500
Earlier we talked about AI-based techniques&nbsp;
that can learn to clone your voice, and then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:28.980
we can perform text to speech. So, it would learn&nbsp;
this. And then, the AI would generate this. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.500
this means that we can write something,&nbsp;
and the AI says it in our voice. So that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:41.040
is text to speech. But get this, what&nbsp;
about speech to gesture? What is that?

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.580
Well, scientists at Ubisoft had a crazy&nbsp;
idea. They said, let’s create a dataset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:54.840
of characters where the AI can see gestures for&nbsp;
agreement, disagreement, being relaxed, neutral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:01.740
scared, and more, learn from it, and apply&nbsp;
it to new virtual characters intelligently.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:09.240
Sounds good, right? But, not so fast. Ubisoft&nbsp;
is not the first to try this idea, here is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:24.900
technique from just two years ago. Look. There&nbsp;
is something here, but we are humans, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:31.860
most of us anyway, and our eyes are highly attuned&nbsp;
to the gestures of other humans, which means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.980
if even the smallest part of the animation&nbsp;
is off, we will immediately notice. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:44.040
think that every single one of you Fellow Scholars&nbsp;
indeed noticed that something is off here. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:50.100
based on the quality of the animation that is&nbsp;
required today to keep the illusion up, I am not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:56.880
sure if this is coming to fruition anytime soon.&nbsp;
Just look at these examples. We are so far away.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.880
But in any case, let’s have a look at the new&nbsp;
technique together. The concept is the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:10.560
first, in goes a speech sample, and it will be&nbsp;
able to generate long sequences using a style of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:27.240
our choosing. Like this. That looks incredible.&nbsp;
Let’s compare it to the previous method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:46.800
Yes, my goodness! A night and day difference!
Such incredible progress in just two years. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.780
this is from only two years ago. And&nbsp;
that would already be pretty cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.620
but, it gets crazier than that. Much crazier.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.720
For instance, we can plug in a new&nbsp;
speaker the AI hasn’t heard about yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:15.900
and this happens. This is really good. But&nbsp;
that’s still nothing. It generalized to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:22.140
only new speakers, but hold on to your&nbsp;
papers, to new languages too. Listen.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:38.040
Wow, and the list of features just keeps on&nbsp;
going. For instance, we can also exert some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:44.160
artistic control here. If we feel that the&nbsp;
hands are too high up, we can lower them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.560
Or, we can even ask for more or less&nbsp;
hip movement during the monologue.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:57.060
And I have to say, it does a lot of things really&nbsp;
well from the lowest energy level gestures, up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.640
the highest. And it still doesn’t stop there, it&nbsp;
is not only better than the previous technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:10.260
is not only more controllable than the previous&nbsp;
technique, but is it also about 7 times faster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.680
and even better, it is done with a neural network&nbsp;
that is significantly simpler than that one.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.440
How many hours of these gestures did&nbsp;
the neural network get access to to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:28.620
learn it? How big was the training set size?&nbsp;
Actually, it requires very little information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:35.880
All it was given was about 2 hours of these&nbsp;
gestures, and it is not copy-pasting, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:42.840
it can continue these gestures for a long-long&nbsp;
monologue seamlessly. And as a cherry on top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:49.200
even some creative control is allowed. So,&nbsp;
these amazing virtual worlds are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.280
be even more full of lifelike&nbsp;
characters, and with this work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:05:00.360
this process is going to be even easier and more&nbsp;
accessible for all of us. What a time to be alive!

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.480
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

